<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.10.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01431</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.10.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>be9d9e1e-875e-4e7f-a6eb-f495d4c03b44</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001">
        <exercise id="c35a4cc7-b899-4f3e-96f6-3425cf6e20d2" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00001">A store receives 2,000 decks of popular trading cards. The number of decks of cards is a function, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, of the number of days, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, since the shipment arrived. Here is a table showing some values of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          
          <table class="os-raise-skinnytable" id="table-00001"><tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row><entry><emphasis><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>d</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></emphasis></entry></row><row><entry>
                  0
                </entry><entry>
                  2,000
                </entry></row><row><entry>
                  5
                </entry><entry>
                  1,283
                </entry></row><row><entry>
                  10
                </entry><entry>
                  823
                </entry></row><row><entry>
                  15
                </entry><entry>
                  528
                </entry></row><row><entry>
                  20
                </entry><entry>
                  338
                </entry></row></tbody></tgroup></table>
          
          <para id="para-00002">Calculate the average rate of change for the following intervals:</para>
            <para id="para-00003">day 0 to day 5</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            
            <para id="para-00004">Try again. Revisit activity 5.10.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00005">The correct answer is –143.4 decks per day.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="e8e769fe-91d9-4b6a-ba07-d1346e99b404" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00006">day 15 to day 20</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            
            <para id="para-00007">Try again. Revisit activity 5.10.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00008">The correct answer is –38 decks per day.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_8af1eca5-26aa-4e3e-a751-2fbe46bf3a21" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00009">A study was conducted to analyze the effects on deer population in a particular area. Let <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> be an exponential function that gives the population of deer <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> years after the study began.

          If <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> and the population is increasing,<emphasis>select three</emphasis> statements that must be true.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            
            <para id="para-00010">Try again. Revisit activities 5.10.1 – 5.10.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00011">The correct answer is A, C, E.
    </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="f850d543-3061-41a4-9114-0324b7017a90" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00012">Function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> models the population, in thousands, of a city <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> years after 1930.</para>
          <para id="para-00013">(<media alt="Graph of a function on a grid."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_12_10_01.svg"/></media>)</para>
          
          <para id="para-00014">The average rate of change of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> from <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>70</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> is approximately 14 thousand people per year.</para>
              <para id="para-00015">Is this value a good way to describe the population change of the city over that time period?</para>

          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            
            <para id="para-00016">Try again. Revisit activity 5.10.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00017">The correct answer is no. Near 1930, the average rate of change was much less than 14 thousand people per year. Approaching the year 2000, the average rate of change was much greater than 14 thousand people per year.
    </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_0ebf5e2b-418d-4722-88e3-a4f74ac76eb9" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para id="para-00018">The rate of change between years 1940 and 1950 is greater than/less than the rate of change between years 1960 and 1970.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            
            <para id="para-00019">Try again. Revisit activity 5.10.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00020">The correct answer is less than. The rate of change increases as the time increases.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="afb5e7e2-0d1f-4b4e-831d-e2385f6acf77" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00021">The function, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, gives the number of copies of a book that have sold <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>w</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> weeks after it was published. The equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>w</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>500</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>w</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> defines this function.<emphasis>Select two</emphasis> domains for which the average rate of change could be a good measure for the number of books sold.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            
            <para id="para-00022">Try again. Revisit activities 5.10.4.</para>
            <para id="para-00023">The correct answer is A, C.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_15f92bae-3f58-40c2-9e38-a85a6f0c7842" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00007">
            <para id="para-00024">The moth population over 10 weeks is graphed below. 
          (<media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_12_10_02.jpg"/></media>) After 4 weeks, the population was 40 thousand. What was the average rate of change from the start to the 4th week? Do not enter commas in your answer.</para>

          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00007">
            
            <para id="para-00025">Try again. Revisit activity 5.10.3. 
    </para>
            <para id="para-00026">The correct answer is –115,000.
    </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="a311646d-b68d-4581-8f58-5b937c8bcf6c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00027">The rate of change between weeks 1 and 2 is greater than/less than the rate of change between weeks 3 and 4.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            
            <para id="para-00028">Try again. Revisit activity 5.10.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00029">The correct answer is greater than. The rate of change decreases as the time increases.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
